Your task is to develop a detailed outline for a one-scene, three-act short story based on this prompt. The whole story takes place in a single scene: one location, one continuous stretch of time.

Work through the following steps in order.

Step 1 — Analysis. Study the writing prompt and work out:
- Key phrases quoted from the prompt, so they stay in view
- Any characters, settings, or situations the prompt names explicitly
- The tone and genre the prompt implies
- Themes that would suit this prompt

Present this analysis inside <brainstorm-1-analysis> tags.

Step 2 — Scenarios. Develop 3 to 5 candidate scenarios suggested by the prompt. For each, give its setting, its central conflict, and the dynamic between its characters. Then choose one and say why it is the strongest.

Present this work inside <brainstorm-2-scenarios> tags.

Steps 1 and 2 are planning notes the author will not publish, so be thorough and follow the interesting thread. It is fine for these sections to run long.

Step 3 — Names. Every character who will appear in the scene but is not named in the writing prompt gets their name from the generate_name tool. Call the tool once for each of them, passing a detailed description of that character as the description argument; the tool requires a non-empty description. Make every call before starting step 4.

Step 4 — Characters. Develop sketches of the 2 to 5 main characters in the scene.

Give each character:
- Full name
- Age
- Physical description
- Key personality traits
- Motivation or goal in this scene
- Relationships to the other characters

Use each character's name exactly as the writing prompt gives it. Adjust the names the tool returns so that family members share a surname and unrelated characters have distinct surnames.

Present the character sketches inside <characters> tags.

Step 5 — Outline. Develop the story beats below, in this order:
1. Inciting incident — the event that sets the scene in motion
2. Rising action 1 — first development and escalation
3. Climax 1 — the first peak of tension
4. Falling action 1 — de-escalation after the first climax
5. Rising action 2 — second development and escalation
6. Climax 2 — the second peak of tension
7. Falling action 2 — de-escalation after the second climax
8. Rising action 3 — third development and escalation
9. Climax 3 — the third peak of tension
10. Falling action 3 — de-escalation after the third climax
11. Resolution — how the scene closes

Describe each beat and explain how it contributes to the overall narrative.

Present the outline inside <outline> tags.

Stop after the outline. A separate later step writes the prose from these materials.

